 r←{x}GetAppFolder filename;appName;home
⍝ Depending on the operating system, the folder where your application may write to is or is not different from
⍝ the folder it is installed in. The installation folder is where the workspace/EXE resides.
⍝ For log files and error snapshots (and possibly the INI file) we need a folder we are allowed to write to.
⍝ This function returns `filename` prefixed by such a folder. The folder is not created.
⍝ * Under Windows it depends on how the application runs:
⍝   * Ordinarily it is `%LOCALAPPDATA%\{appName}\`
⍝   * When running as a Windows Service it is the folder of `⎕WSID`, which is where the workspace was
⍝     loaded from, or the EXE in case of a bound EXE.\\
⍝     Services usually run under the system account, which has no useful `%LOCALAPPDATA%`.
⍝ * Under Linux it is `$XDG_DATA_HOME/{appName}/`, or `~/.local/share/{appName}/` when XDG_DATA_HOME is not set
⍝ * Under macOS it is `~/Library/Application Support/{appName}/`
⍝ `appName` is taken from [CONFIG]AppName.
⍝ Pass `G` as left argument in a test case; in a running server `G` is a local of `Run`.
 :If 0<⎕NC'x'  ⍝ Special case: test cases!
     ⎕SHADOW'G'
     G←x
 :EndIf
 appName←⊃G.INI.Get'CONFIG:AppName'
 :Select 1 ⎕C ##.APLTreeUtils2.GetOperatingSystem ⍬
 :Case 'WIN'
     :If IsRunningAsService
         r←(⊃1 ⎕NPARTS ⎕WSID),filename
     :Else
         r←((2 ⎕NQ #'GetEnvironment' 'LOCALAPPDATA'),'\',appName,'\'),filename
     :EndIf
 :Case 'LIN'
     home←2 ⎕NQ #'GetEnvironment' 'XDG_DATA_HOME'
     :If 0=≢home
         home←(2 ⎕NQ #'GetEnvironment' 'HOME'),'/.local/share'
     :EndIf
     r←(home,'/',appName,'/'),filename
 :Case 'MAC'
     r←((2 ⎕NQ #'GetEnvironment' 'HOME'),'/Library/Application Support/',appName,'/'),filename
 :Else
     11 ⎕SIGNAL⍨'Operating system not supported'
 :EndSelect
⍝Done
